{"causes":["The Mamluk Sultanate's restriction on Jewish education and intellectual pursuits in Egypt led to a diaspora of Jewish scholars seeking refuge in Europe.","The rise of Ashkenazi Judaism in Eastern Europe created an environment conducive to the development of Kabbalistic thought.","The influx of Sephardic Jews fleeing persecution in Spain and Portugal brought new ideas and perspectives that enriched Kabbalistic literature."],"effects":["Kabbalistic thought became a distinct school of Jewish mysticism, characterized by its emphasis on divine emanations and the interconnectedness of all things.","The Zohar's influence extended beyond Jewish communities, with echoes appearing in Christian Gnosticism and Hermeticism.","A new wave of Kabbalistic scholarship emerged in the 18th century, revitalizing interest in the text and paving the way for its continued study."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Rabbi Moshe de Leon","sourceName":"Rabbi Moshe de Leon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kabbalah","targetName":"Kabbalah","context":"The Zohar is a key work in the development of Kabbalistic thought."},{"sourceSlug":"Mamluk Sultanate","sourceName":"Mamluk Sultanate","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jewish Education","targetName":"Jewish Education","context":"The Mamluk Sultanate's restriction on Jewish education led to a diaspora of Jewish scholars."},{"sourceSlug":"Ashkenazi Judaism","sourceName":"Ashkenazi Judaism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kabbalistic Thought","targetName":"Kabbalistic Thought","context":"The rise of Ashkenazi Judaism created an environment conducive to the development of Kabbalistic thought."},{"sourceSlug":"Sephardic Jews","sourceName":"Sephardic Jews","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kabbalistic Literature","targetName":"Kabbalistic Literature","context":"The influx of Sephardic Jews enriched Kabbalistic literature with new ideas and perspectives."}],"places":[{"name":"Pergamon, Turkey","role":"Place of exile"},{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Center of Kabbalistic scholarship"},{"name":"Lyon, France","role":"Location of Zohar's earliest printed edition"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Zohar","type":"Reference article","slug":"zohar"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q205388","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zohar"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Zohar contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Zohar (Hebrew: זֹהַר, Zōhar, 'Splendour' or 'Radiance') is the foundational text of Kabbalah (Jewish mysticism), a mystical commentary on the Torah written primarily in Aramaic and composed c. 1280–1286 CE by the Spanish Kabbalist Moses de León (c. 1240–1305), though attributed in the text to the 2nd-century CE Tanna Shimon bar Yochai. First appearing in Spain in the 1280s, the Zohar consists of several discrete works — the main body of the Zohar (Midrash ha-Zohar), the Sifra di-Tsni'uta ('Book of Concealment'), the Idra Rabbā ('Great Assembly'), and several other sections — most presenting themselves as discourses of Shimon bar Yochai and his circle on the mystical meaning of the Torah.

The Zohar is the supreme work of Jewish mystical theology — its central subject is the nature of the divine (Ein Sof, 'Without Limit' — the infinite and unknowable Godhead) and the ten Sefirot (divine emanations or attributes through which the Ein Sof manifests and creates): Keter (Crown), Ḥokhmah (Wisdom), Binah (Understanding), Ḥesed (Loving-kindness), Gevurah (Strength/Judgment), Tiferet (Beauty), Netzach (Eternity), Hod (Splendour), Yesod (Foundation), Malkhut (Kingdom). The Zohar's theology of divine emanation provides a mystical cosmology in which the entire universe is a manifestation of the divine, Torah is the name of God, and human action (especially the observance of the commandments — mitzvot) affects the harmony of the divine Sefirot.

The Zohar was accepted by most of the Jewish world as authentic from the 14th century, achieving near-canonical status comparable to the Talmud in some communities — particularly after the expulsion from Spain (1492) and the development of the Safed Kabbalah (Luria, Cordovero, Vital) in the 16th century. Through Christian Kabbalah (Pico della Mirandola, Johannes Reuchlin) it also influenced the Renaissance esoteric tradition and, through later occultism (the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn), 20th-century Western esotericism.","subjects":["Jewish Literature","Medieval Era","Jewish Mysticism","Kabbalah","Sefirot","Torah Commentary","Moses de León","Esoteric Tradition"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Zohar's influence on Jewish mysticism and its transmission through various esoteric traditions make it a crucial work in understanding the development of Kabbalistic thought.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
